The '''History of Sarta (1906–1947)''' covers the era from the consolidation of the [[Partido Nacional]] after 1906 to the collapse of the wartime fascist order in 1947. It began with a conventional parliamentary party system, passed through a short reformist opening in 1910–1912, and then hardened into an authoritarian one-party state after [[Jaime Checa]]'s coup. The kingdom became more industrial, more urban, and more repressive over time, while its foreign policy tightened around Volisania and the broader fascist bloc.

== 1906–1910: National Party rule ==

After the [[Partido Nacional]] won the lower house in 1906, Sarta entered a period of traditional party government. The party still behaved like a conventional parliamentary force: it balanced the interests of the royal companies, the old landowning elite, and the commercial districts that had benefited most from industrial growth. Its leaders presented themselves as defenders of order after the turmoil of the late nineteenth century, and they tried to contain labour unrest without surrendering the basic structure of elite rule.

That order was under strain. The global [[Second War of the Isthmus#The Mild Depression|Mild Depression]] depressed trade in the wake of the war, and Volisanian imports no longer provided the same stimulus to Sartan coal and shipping. Layoffs spread through mining, rail, port, and workshop employment, especially in the coastal cities and company towns. Socialists, labour organisers, and reformist newspapers gained ground among workers who felt that the industrial boom had enriched a narrow elite while leaving wages and housing insecure.

The government remained committed to company-led development, but the balance was changing. Some enterprises were crown companies; others were formally private or chartered, yet still run by royal relatives, senior lords, or allied merchant houses. The resulting system concentrated ownership, public contracts, and land access in a small circle. It also tied the new urban economy more closely to the political fortunes of the National Party itself.

== 1910–1912: the progressive spark ==

The 1910 election ended the National Party's initial dominance and brought a socialist coalition to power. The coalition united workers' unions with the Socialist Party and pressed for a wider democratic order. Parliament was universalised by extending the vote to all adult citizens, not just the older Petran male electorate, and the new government tried to bring state-run enterprises under stronger public oversight.

The reform programme was ambitious. Its supporters wanted the state to be less a machine for elite extraction and more a vehicle for public administration. They pushed to reorganise the royal companies, tighten parliamentary supervision of transport and mining, and use state enterprise to support wages, housing, and urban services. The two-year reform period was later remembered as a progressive spark because it changed the language of politics almost overnight.

The pace of change also produced a reaction. Conservatives, royalists, and industrial elites feared that the coalition would use the constitution to weaken the monarchy or abolish it altogether. Petran Catholics, who had long been the kingdom's dominant religious community, also feared that a radical turn could leave them vulnerable to retaliation if the balance of power shifted too far.

== 1912–1913: coup and royal restoration ==

In 1912, [[Jaime Checa]] seized the moment with a coup carried out in collaboration with Volisania's [[National Volisan Party]]. The coup was justified publicly as a defence of the monarchy and of public order. Checa and his allies argued that the socialist coalition was preparing to rewrite the constitution and remove the king, and they presented themselves as the only force capable of preventing revolutionary collapse.

The coup was initially welcomed by conservative voters, royalists, and business interests who wanted the socialist reforms stopped. Checa was made prime minister under a new legislature that banned all parties not aligned with the National Party and its Volisanian patrons. The freedoms of speech and assembly that had been broadened in 1910 were quickly narrowed again, and the legal opening of the progressive spark closed almost as soon as it had begun.

The old king backed the coup, but his role did not last long. He died in 1913, removing the monarchy's last strong personal anchor and leaving Checa and his allies to rule by party, police, and patronage rather than by royal authority alone. The death also deepened the sense that the coup had not restored stability so much as replaced one crisis with another.

== 1913–1935: authoritarian consolidation ==

Checa's government gradually abandoned the language of constitutional rescue and moved toward open authoritarianism. Sarta's political life became more tightly controlled, and the regime built a closer relationship with Volisania's fascist circles. Collaboration was not only ideological. It also involved military coordination, finance, and industrial planning. Volisanian capital and credit helped drive the kingdom's attempt to catch up industrially, but the catch-up came with harsher labour control and greater dependence on the state.

The economy shifted away from extraction and toward manufacturing, transport, ship repair, and military supply. Coal remained important, but the larger goal became industrial depth: machine shops, metalworking, rail-linked depots, and port infrastructure were expanded to serve both domestic order and external war. This change accelerated urbanisation, especially around the coast and the major cities, but it also made workers more dependent on employers, housing systems, and state-linked firms.

Land ownership followed the same logic. Estates, urban parcels, and industrial land were increasingly concentrated among families tied to the crown, the party, or the new state-industrial network. That concentration made it easier to build docks, warehouses, and worker housing, but it also sharpened labour conflict. Workers saw that political power, land, and enterprise were now being pooled in the same hands, and resentment spread through mines, ports, and factories.

Repression widened alongside growth. The regime established internment camps for Sabians, socialists, communists, and LGBT people, and it treated political opposition as a security problem rather than a public disagreement. Censorship, surveillance, and police pressure replaced the more open debate of the 1910–1912 reform period. Zamarran Catholics became a particular target from the 1930s, as the state increasingly read their religious ties to Kastruneto and the wider Zamarran world as a political threat.

Border tensions also persisted. Sarta clashed intermittently with Echia and Kastruneto, and in 1923 the [[Sartan-Jacan affair]] nearly triggered war after a plan to seize the mouth of the Rio Grande leaked and forced Jacan to sever relations. By then, the regime had become accustomed to ruling through intimidation, foreign alignment, and the promise of national revival rather than through broad legitimacy.

== 1935–1944: war abroad, unrest at home ==

The [[Great Adonian War]] gave Checa's regime its largest external stage. Sarta joined Volisania in the opening campaigns against Zong Balboa in 1935, and Sartan soldiers were sent overseas both to the Balboa front and to the [[United Midlands]], where they assisted Mercia against Louyang and Sinchew in the Great Lurandian Range. At first, the deployments were popular. They were presented as proof that Sarta had recovered its military confidence and was now a full partner in fascist strategy.

That approval did not last. The war dragged on, casualties mounted, and the costs of mobilisation became visible at home. In 1940, the National Party added a new ambition: the invasion of Kastruneto to decapitate the Zamarran Church. The attack rapidly drew western Kastruneto into the conflict and turned what had been a foreign war into a more direct northern frontier struggle.

By then, internal opposition had been growing for years. The [[Movimiento Unido]] had been carrying out guerrilla operations in the Sabean highlands and, at intervals, in urban districts for roughly two decades. Its activity escalated after the Kastruneto invasion, as civilians grew tired of fighting for Volisania and of living under emergency rule. The movement drew support from socialists, liberals, and other opponents of the regime, and it increasingly presented itself as the only alternative to permanent war.

Checa's own authority weakened as he aged and developed Alzheimer's disease. Factionalism spread inside the National Party, and the government increasingly had to choose between competing hardliners and local powerbrokers. Assassination attempts became more frequent. On 11 March 1944, Checa died, and a loyalist successor replaced him, but the party was already too fractured to recover its old discipline.

== 1944–1947: famine, collapse, and surrender ==

The situation worsened sharply in late 1944. The [[Movimiento de Restauració]] in eastern Volisania cut supply lines that had sustained the Sartan war economy, and a famine began to spread through the kingdom. The disruption hit both the cities and the military zones. Industrial districts lost imports, food prices rose, and the wartime state that had relied on constant movement of men and material began to falter.

That crisis deepened in May 1947, when Volisania's government was toppled on 4 May and halted all further support for Sarta. In the south, Kastruneto forces launched a three-month push into the Rio Grande region, coordinating with the [[Movimiento Unido]] to close in on [[Mariapolis]]. The Sartan leadership surrendered there on 18 July 1947, ending the wartime regime.

By the surrender at Mariapolis, Sarta had become more urban and more industrial than in 1906, but the gains of industrial catch-up had been swallowed by repression, war, and famine. The kingdom's political system had moved from parliamentary caution to authoritarian collaboration and then into collapse under the pressure of total war. The postwar settlement would have to rebuild a state that had spent three decades trading legitimacy for control. The years that followed were marked by war tribunals, the discovery of atrocities, and a difficult reconciliation with the rest of the world.

